================================================================================
New York City Taxi and Limousine Commission trip record data
================================================================================

No TLC trip record is redistributed in this repository.

The exploratory analysis and the fitted Brooklyn parameters derive from the New
York City Taxi and Limousine Commission's High Volume For-Hire Vehicle trip
records, together with the TLC's taxi zone lookup table and taxi zone shapefile.
These are published by the TLC at

    https://www.nyc.gov/site/tlc/about/tlc-trip-record-data.page

and are subject to the TLC's own terms of use, which govern their use and
redistribution and are not altered by this project's license.

**How to obtain the source data.** `eda/fetch_data.py` downloads the pinned
files and records each one's size and SHA-256 digest. Each is compared against
`eda/raw-digests.toml`, which is tracked and holds the bytes the fitted
artifacts were made from, so a file that differs from them is reported:

    python eda/fetch_data.py

The files land in `data/raw/`, which this repository does not track.

**What this repository does carry.** `datasets/nyc-brooklyn/artifacts/` holds
aggregate statistics computed from those records — a per-cell travel-duration
table of percentile values, a mean distance per origin-destination pair, a
demand rate table, and a day-type table. They contain no trip record, no
timestamp, no route, and no vehicle, driver, license or company identifier. Each
value is a count or a rate over an origin, destination, hour and day-type cell,
computed across 7,089,190 requests for the demand table; the rarest cells rest
on a single journey.

`config/zoning/` is not tracked by this repository. `eda/fit_travel.py` writes
the fitted Brooklyn place map there when it runs, and `eda/zoning.py` regenerates
the two lookup tables that group the TLC's taxi-zone identifiers by borough and
by service zone. The provenance of the artifacts above — the source window, the
exclusions applied, the day counts, and a digest of each artifact — is recorded
in `datasets/nyc-brooklyn/artifacts/PROVENANCE-20-v1.toml` and
`PROVENANCE-demand-20-v1.toml`, and the code that produces them is
`eda/fit_travel.py` and `eda/fit_demand.py`.

The fleet described by these inputs is a hypothetical operation defined for
study purposes. The artifacts record demand and travel conditions observed in
Brooklyn. They do not describe an existing service.

